<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The policy every Electron child process is signed with, EXCEPT the ones
  scripts/sign-macos.mjs routes elsewhere (the Rust sidecar, the Speech helper,
  the shortcut helper, and the generic "<Product> Helper" that hosts Chromium's
  utility services — see entitlements.mac.helper.plist).

  In practice this file is the GPU, Renderer and Plugin helpers' policy. It used
  to be a copy of the main app's set, which handed all three of them the
  microphone, unscoped Apple Events, and disabled library validation. None of
  the three has a use for any of that:

  - The Renderer helper runs web content. `getUserMedia` there is brokered by
    Chromium's audio service; the renderer sandbox never opens the device
    itself, so the microphone entitlement bought nothing and widened what a
    compromised renderer inherits.
  - Nothing in Plainsong drives another application through Apple Events from a
    child process. The main app holds that entitlement for the two bundles
    named in its own plist.
  - Library validation is only disabled to load unsigned native code. These
    helpers load the Electron Framework, which carries the same Team ID.

  What remains is what Chromium genuinely requires: V8 needs JIT and writable-
  executable memory, and `inherit` is what makes a child adopt the parent's
  sandbox rather than being denied outright.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.inherit</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
</plist>
